, Minnesota, Montana, Nebraska, North Dakota, and Utah. Centerspace was established in 1970 and incorporated in North Dakota.","ceo_bio":"Ms. Anne M. Olson Esq. | President, CEO, Secretary & Executive Trustee | Age 48 | Compensation: $1.7M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 334 people. Revenue per employee of $819K ranks in the below the sector median among Real Estate peers. Each employee generates $192K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $10K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 2\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (1/10).","financial_summary":["D/B/A Centerspace is a small-cap real estate business with a $2.0B market cap on $274M of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +2% over the past three years and +14% annually across the full decade, which puts it in mature, slow-growth territory. Operating margins are contracting by -25.0pp, the first warning that competitive pressure is eroding the franchise.","Returns on capital run at 2% against a 7% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests.","The business throws off $64M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 23 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 5.76\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, debt is heavy at 5.6\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and leverage like that is what turns a bad year into a permanent loss, and thin 1.4\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Shareholder yield of 2.8% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Operating margins contracting -25.0pp YoY \u2014 competitive pressure is eroding the franchise","sev":"red","cat":"trend"},{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (0.9) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Above-average short interest at 7.0% of float","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Interest-rate sensitivity and tenant-credit risk \u2014 cap rates and refinancing costs move faster than the 10-year leases can absorb.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Remote work structural vacancy \u2014 the post-pandemic shift to hybrid work has permanently impaired demand for urban office space across most markets.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Climate and insurance cost escalation \u2014 rising premiums and coverage withdrawals in flood/fire zones are repricing assets that the market once treated as perpetuities.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["28 consecutive years of dividend payments \u2014 an operating record that makes the dividend claim credible","Healthy payout ratio leaves room for both dividend growth and reinvestment","Minimal goodwill on the balance sheet \u2014 low impairment risk"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"Analysts\u2019 Price Target Changes for November 10th (AIG, AIZ, ALL, ANGI, ATTO, CE, CEVA, CHRD, CPT, CSR)","source":"thelincolnianonline.com","link":"https://www.thelincolnianonline.com/2026/11/10/analysts-price-target-changes-for-november-10th-aig-aiz-all-angi-atto-ce-ceva-chrd-cpt-csr.html","date":"2026-11-10","timestamp":1794347084.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.557,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":["analyst_action"]},{"title":"Why (CSR) Price Action Is Critical for Tactical Trading","source":"news.stocktradersdaily.com","link":"https://news.stocktradersdaily.c